<resources>
    <!-- The chassis has no notification or battery indicator LED. -->
    <integer name="config_deviceLightCapabilities">0</integer>

    <!-- config_deviceHardwareKeys and config_deviceHardwareWakeKeys stay at the
         lineage-sdk default of 64 (volume rocker only, no capacitive keys), and
         both bits are correct.

         Do NOT clear the wake bit. Earlier revisions of this project recorded
         that "matrix keys cannot wake this SoC at any configuration", on the
         reasoning that MTK's kpd_pdrv_suspend() writes KP_EN=0 so the keypad
         block cannot assert its SPM wake source. The premise was right and the
         conclusion was wrong: the block is only disabled BECAUSE of that write,
         and rootdir/etc/init/hw/init.mt6755.rc now sets
         /sys/bus/platform/drivers/mtk-kpd/kpd_call_state to 2, which makes both
         the suspend and resume hooks skip it. KP_EN stays 1 across suspend.

         Confirmed on the handset, end to end:
           * nine suspend/resume cycles logged the call_status == 2 branch and
             never "KEYPAD is disabled" (E-028)
           * R12_KP_IRQ_B is bit 2 of the SPM R12 wake mask
             (spm_v2/mt_spm_internal.c wakesrc_str[]), and the live suspend mask
             0x1e86c5e7 has it set
           * the owner pressed Volume Up with the screen off and the handset
             woke
         So "volume buttons wake device" in Settings works on this build.

         The bit is load-bearing for a second reason as well: it also gates
         mVolumeAnswerCall in PhoneWindowManager.updateSettings()
         (frameworks/base .../policy/PhoneWindowManager.java:2451), while
         LineageParts hides that toggle on a different condition entirely -
         hasVolumeKeys plus voice capability (ButtonSettings:371). Clearing the
         wake bit would silently kill answer-on-volume while leaving its switch
         on screen. -->
</resources>
